diagnosis_text:
A comprehensive four-phase diagnostic sweep found no fault anywhere in the Kath4 topology. Phase 1's topology-wide reachability probe returned zero packet loss across all 28 tested cross-subnet host pairs, meaning traffic is actively flowing through both campus gateways (mswitch0 serving VLANs 10/20/30 at 172.16.x.254, and sshswitch serving VLANs 5/6 at 10.x.0.254). Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) confirmed that all 15 end-hosts hold valid IP addresses, default routes, and ARP entries marking their gateways as REACHABLE; no ACL/nftables drop rules were present on any device, and no duplicate MACs exist. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) showed all nine FRR routers fully converged — every OSPF neighbor in Full/DR or Full/Backup state with 17 routes apiece — and no traffic-control shaping on any interface. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no resource pressure or stress processes on any device. The most plausible explanation for the helpdesk report is a transient OSPF reconvergence event that occurred immediately after the reported device reload: during the brief period before adjacencies re-established, hosts in one or both campus halves would have lacked a working route to their gateway subnets. That transient has since fully resolved with no residual fault. No corrective action is required; the network is fully operational.
=== END ===